# Keep the toolchain byte-clean across platforms.
#
# Check every text file out with LF endings, whatever the user's git config
# says. A CRLF checkout (core.autocrlf on Windows or WSL) puts a \r on every
# line, and the shell scripts fail on it (`set -e\r` is an illegal option).
# word itself reads a \r as whitespace, in .w source and in .s files, but LF
# keeps every file the same bytes on every platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The committed `word` binary is the reproducible seed (docs/BOOTSTRAP.md). Mark
# it, and the other binary files, so git never converts line endings in them.
word binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary

# GitHub's Linguist has no entry for word (adding one takes a pull request to
# github-linguist/linguist), so it detects `*.w` as CWeb, the other language
# that uses the extension, and counts it in the repo's Languages bar.
# `linguist-vendored` leaves the files out of that bar without changing diffs.
# `linguist-language` sets the highlighting GitHub uses for each file.
# JavaScript is the closest grammar to word's punctuation: `//` comments,
# `== != && || !`, `%`, `{key: value}` literals and `arr[i]` indexing all match.
# `if`, `else`, `return`, `break`, `true`, `false` and `null` are JavaScript
# keywords too, and word's `loop` and `none` show as plain identifiers.
*.w linguist-vendored linguist-language=JavaScript

# Leave every file out of the Languages bar. The test suites are shell and the
# second compiler in bootstrap/ is Python, so the bar showed a shell project.
# This only changes the language statistics, not highlighting or diffs.
* linguist-detectable=false
